alues">%1$s / %2$s / %3$s</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_points_of">%1$s / %2$d</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_budget">%1$s× budget</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_per_thousand">%1$s per 1,000 frames</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_millis">%1$s ms</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_megabytes">%1$s MB</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_count">%1$s</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_pass">pass</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_fail">fail</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_partly">waiting</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_value_target_at_most">at most %1$s</string>\n\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_janky_frames">Janky frames</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_ui_p90">Main-thread time p90</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_ui_p99">Main-thread time p99</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_slow_frames">Slow frames</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_frozen_frames">Frozen frames</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_long_work">Long main-thread work</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_cold_start">Cold start</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_warm_start">Warm start</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_app_memory">App memory (PSS less graphics)</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_blocking_gc">Blocking garbage collections</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_strict_mode">StrictMode violations</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_minified">R8</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_baseline_profile">Baseline profile</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_metric_not_debuggable">Not debuggable</string>\n\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_janky_frames">Open Janky frames by screen and start with the worst screen. The usual causes: recomposition on every frame of an animation (read animated values in graphicsLayer, offset or drawBehind lambdas), alpha on a full-screen layer, work on the main thread during a transition, and a first frame that builds a whole screen. Record a trace with the android-profiler skill to see which.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_ui_p90">Ordinary frames keep the main thread busy for most of the frame budget, which leaves no room for a slower one. Look for layouts measured twice (intrinsics, nested weights), lists without contentType, and work in composition that belongs in remember or the draw phase.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_ui_p99">The slowest frames keep the main thread busy past the frame budget. These are usually first frames: a screen built for the first time, or a list item type seen for the first time. Make sure the baseline profile covers them, keep first frames small, and defer what the first frame does not show.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_slow_frames">Frames over 16 ms miss even a 60 Hz budget. Find them in Frame times and record a trace of that moment.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_frozen_frames">A frame took longer than 700 ms: the app froze. Something blocked the main thread: disk or network on it (check StrictMode below), a large decode, or a lock. Move it to a background dispatcher.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_long_work">The main thread did more than 50 ms of work for single frames. StrictMode below names disk and network calls; a trace names the rest. Defer what is not needed for the first frame.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_cold_start">Start-up does too much before the first frame. Keep Application.onCreate and the first screen\\\'s creation free of I/O and of eager singletons, make sure the baseline profile is compiled, and measure with tools/perf/perf.sh coldstart on devRelease.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_warm_start">Re-creating the activity is slow: the first screen does work on creation that it could keep in its ViewModel, or restores more state than it needs.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_app_memory">The app keeps more memory than the 58.4 MB budget. Look for caches without a bound, images decoded at full size, and screens that hold their data after they close. A heap dump from the profiler shows who owns it.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_blocking_gc">The garbage collector stopped every thread. That comes from allocating fast: objects created on every frame, in draw lambdas or animation callbacks. Hoist them into remember or a node.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_strict_mode">The main thread touched the disk or the network. The examples below name where; move each call to a background dispatcher, or into a repository that already runs there.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_minified">This build is not shrunk by R8, which also optimises the code. Release builds are; measure one: devRelease.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_baseline_profile">ART has not compiled this build with the baseline profile, so its first use of each screen runs interpreted. On a debuggable build it never will; install devRelease. Otherwise run tools/perf/perf.sh, or adb shell cmd package compile -f -m speed-profile.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_fix_not_debuggable">A debuggable build turns off most of ART\\\'s compilation, so it is several times slower than the release. Its numbers are pessimistic; measure devRelease.</string>\n\n    <string name="perfhud_report_title">Performance report: %1$s %2$s (%3$s)</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_report_generated">Generated %1$s on %2$s %3$s, Android %4$s (API %5$d), display %6$s Hz.</string>\n    <string name="perfhud_report_recorded">Recorded %1$s of use.</string>\n</resources>\n'

